ree dragons is valuable. Losing one forfeits potential Two Dragon Pungs (6 pts) or even Little Three Dragons (64 pts) if you get pungs.\n\nSo do NOT discard any dragon unless necessary.\n\nSimilarly, B5/B6/B8 are paired \u2014 strong foundation.\n\nTherefore, safest and most strategic discard is **W9** \u2014 a terminal with no connections.\n\nAlternatively, **W2** \u2014 also weak.\n\nBut W9 is more dangerous \u2014 higher chance someone needs it for 7-8-9 chow. W2 is less commonly sought.\n\nUnshown: W9 has 4 left \u2014 all unseen, meaning no one has played or used it \u2014 suspicious? Possibly someone waiting for it.\n\nWhereas W2: 4 unshown, but W2 was played by across player (Play W1 twice), but not W2.\n\nStill, better to discard **W9** \u2014 slightly riskier, but both are bad.\n\nAnother angle: **Play J2**? No \u2014 why break dragon symmetry?\n\nNo.\n\nBest: preserve all pairs and dragons, dump an isolated terminal.\n\nW2 and W9 are both candidates.\n\nBut W2 is number 2 \u2014 can form 1-2-3 or 2-3-4. Slightly more useful than W9.\n\nW9 can only be in 7-8-9.\n\nAnd 7-8-9 is common \u2014 previous player has T6-T7-T8, across has W3-W4-W5, so likely others building sequences.\n\nMoreover, I have B7-B8-B9 \u2014 I don\u2019t need W9.\n\nSo **W9 is the weakest tile**.\n\nReason: It is a terminal tile with limited use, no support in hand, and potentially dangerous if an opponent is completing a 7-8-9 chow in Characters. Discarding it minimizes damage to my own prospects while removing a liability.\n\nAnswer: Play W9"}
