ively, focusing on **Half Flush** (6 pts) in Dots (B) is possible, especially with [B7,B8,B9] already melded and B5/B6/B8 remaining.\n\nReason: Among the options, **Play J2** stands out because:\n- I hold only one of each dragon; there's no immediate way to complete a pung without drawing duplicates.\n- J2 appears less dangerous than J1/J3 since J1 and J3 were played earlier by others (across player Play J1; unshown count shows J2 still has 4 available \u2014 meaning none drawn into melds yet).\n- However, crucially, **no other player has shown interest in dragons**, making them relatively safe to discard.\n- Retaining B5/B6/B8 allows flexibility toward **Middle Tiles** (24 pts) or **Pure Straight** (16 pts) if I can build chows like [B4,B5,B6], [B1,B2,B3], etc., though this is speculative.\n- Yet, continuing to hold all three unique dragons blocks hand efficiency. Sacrificing one (J2) minimizes loss while freeing up space.\n\nDiscarding an honor tile is better than breaking a duplicate suited pair at this stage. Between J1/J2/J3, J2 is slightly safer based on activity (others haven't played it), and I see no immediate threat.\n\nAnswer: Play J2"}
